=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — the three core routers (router_core_1, router_core_2, router_core_3) continuously dropping and re-establishing OSPF adjacencies — is not occurring in the live network. Every diagnostic dimension checked over a ~7-minute observation window confirms the network is fully healthy.

All nine OSPF adjacencies in the topology are in Full/DR or Full/Backup state and have been continuously stable since initial formation approximately 40 seconds after lab startup. Neighbor uptime counters grew monotonically through every check (1m → 2m36s → 5m56s) with no resets. The neighbor detail shows exactly 6 state changes per pair, which is the normal Down→Init→2Way→ExStart→Exchange→Loading→Full progression from a single formation event.

OSPF hello and dead intervals are identical across all devices (Hello 10 s, Dead 40 s) with no per-interface overrides and no mismatches. No BFD daemon is running. No authentication is configured. Interface statistics show zero errors and zero dropped packets on all core-to-core links. No traffic-control (tc) rules introduce artificial loss. No cron job or background script was found that could periodically kill or restart ospfd. The FRR log contains only startup and convergence entries. Routing tables are complete and stable on all three core routers, and end-to-end data-plane reachability from host_1_1_1_1 to the server-farm web servers is confirmed at 0% packet loss.

The helpdesk ticket describes a problem that does not exist in the current lab state. The network is healthy, fully converged, and no corrective action is required.
